  "question": {
    "text": "For a moving coil galvanometer, the deflection in the coil is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>05</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>rad</mi></math>&nbsp;when a current of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>10</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>mA</mi></math> is passed through it. If the torsional constant of suspension wire is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>4</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#215;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>&#8211;</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><msup><mi>rad</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></math>, the magnetic field is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>01</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></math> and the number of turns in the coil is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>200</mn></math>, the area of each turn (in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi>cm</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></math> ) is :",
    "image": null
  },
